@rem Exercises for lesson 05_operators (C++ track). Format: docs/CONTENT_FORMAT.md

@exercise cpp-op-theory-friend
@type theory
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 2
@prompt
operator<< for cout cannot be a member of your class. Which keyword lets such a free function access the private fields?
@answer
friend
@explain
A friend declaration inside the class gives the free function access to the private members.

@exercise cpp-op-choice-postfix
@type choice
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Which declaration is the POSTFIX increment (c++) of class Clock?
@choices
- Clock& operator++();
* Clock operator++(int);
- Clock operator++(Clock);
- void operator++(c);
@explain
The unnamed int parameter only tells the compiler that this is the postfix version; the prefix version takes no parameter.

@exercise cpp-op-choice-overload
@type choice
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Can two functions in the same scope differ only in their return type?
@choices
- Yes, the compiler looks at the return type
* No, the parameter lists must differ
- Yes, but only for member functions
- Yes, if one is const
@explain
Overload resolution uses the number and types of parameters, not the return type.

@exercise cpp-op-predict-plus
@type predict
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Counter {
public:
    int n;
    Counter(int v) : n(v) { }
    Counter operator+(const Counter& o) const { return Counter(n + o.n); }
};

int main() {
    Counter a(3), b(4);
    Counter c = a + b;
    cout << c.n << " " << a.n << endl;
    return 0;
}
@answer
7 3
@explain
a + b is a.operator+(b): it builds a NEW Counter with 3 + 4. The operands are unchanged.

@exercise cpp-op-predict-prefix-postfix
@type predict
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 3
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Num {
public:
    int v;
    Num(int x) : v(x) { }
    Num& operator++() { ++v; return *this; }
    Num operator++(int) { Num old = *this; ++v; return old; }
};

int main() {
    Num a(5);
    Num b = a++;
    Num c = ++a;
    cout << a.v << " " << b.v << " " << c.v << endl;
    return 0;
}
@answer
7 5 7
@explain
a++ returns the OLD value: b is 5 and a becomes 6. ++a increments first: a becomes 7 and c gets 7.

@exercise cpp-op-fill-operator-plus
@type fill
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Name the member function that makes a + b work for Vec objects.
@code
class Vec {
public:
    int x, y;
    Vec(int a, int b) : x(a), y(b) { }
    Vec ___(const Vec& o) const {
        return Vec(x + o.x, y + o.y);
    }
};
@answer
operator+
@explain
a + b is translated to a.operator+(b).

@exercise cpp-op-fill-prefix-return
@type fill
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Complete the prefix increment. It should return a reference to the object itself.
@code
class Num {
public:
    int v;
    Num(int x) : v(x) { }
    Num& operator++() {
        ++v;
        return ___;
    }
};
@answer
*this
@explain
this is a pointer to the current object, so *this is the object; returning a reference to it is the usual convention.

@exercise cpp-op-fill-postfix-param
@type fill
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Complete the declaration of the postfix increment.
@code
class Num {
public:
    int v;
    Num(int x) : v(x) { }
    Num operator++(___) {
        Num old = *this;
        ++v;
        return old;
    }
};
@answer
int
@explain
The int parameter is unused; it only distinguishes the postfix form from the prefix form.
